I am not sure that

y1_f <- bf(y1 | trials(n1) ~ (1 | p | group))
y2_f <- bf(y2 | trials(n2) ~ (1 | p | group))

does what I need. As far as I understand this is for the case when intercept and slope have a correlation structure. In my case the correlation structure is imposed by the random effects. I only have an intercept in the model and nothing else. What I would like to obtain is something like the result that you get with res_cor in the multivariate case.

Could something like the following procedure work?

1. fit the model with the intercept only and extract the raw residuals
2. use the residuals as the group variable and fit the model again

I am not sure if I have explain my problem properly.

Many thanks

On Thursday, 18 January 2018 10:10:24 UTC, Paul Buerkner wrote:

> 1.
>
> brms cannot yet handle missing values on the fly, but it is on my ToDo list (see https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/issues/27).
